[[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]] entered the competition in the third round, receiving a [[Bye (sports)|bye]] as a Football League Championship club. They were drawn against [[Southern Football League|Southern League Division One Midlands]] side [[Chasetown F.C.|Chasetown]], who had become the lowest-ranked side ever to reach the third round of the competition after defeating [[Port Vale F.C.|Port Vale]] in the second round.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2008/jan/05/newsstory.facup1 |title=Welcome to Chasetown, six divisions and a world away |newspaper=The Guardian |last=James |first=Stuart |date=4 January 2008 |access-date=1 October 2020}}</ref> The difference in league positions between the two sides was also the largest in the tournament's history.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://lichfieldlive.co.uk/2011/12/20/chasetown-fc-forward-to-make-the-draw-for-fa-cup-fourth-round/ |title=Chasetown FC forward to make the draw for FA Cup Fourth Round |publisher=Lichfield Live |last=Goddard |first=Dave |date=20 December 2011 |access-date=1 October 2020}}</ref> Cardiff were given an early scare as an [[own goal]] by defender [[Kevin McNaughton]], from a cross by Ben Steane, gave Chasetown the lead in the 17th minute. Midfielder [[Peter Whittingham]] scored a first half [[injury time]] equaliser for Cardiff. Teenager [[Aaron Ramsey]], making his first appearance in an FA Cup match, put Cardiff ahead with a close-range [[Header (association football)|header]] and winger [[Paul Parry]] hit the ball through Chasetown goalkeeper Lee Evans' legs to ensure progression to the fourth round.<ref name="bbcchase">{{cite news |title=Chasetown 1–3 Cardiff|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/7163953.stm|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=5 January 2008|access-date=31 August 2015}}</ref> Cardiff were drawn away to [[EFL League Two|League Two]] side [[Hereford United F.C.|Hereford United]] at [[Edgar Street]]. McNaughton opened the scoring with his first goal for Cardiff striking a volley from the edge of the Hereford [[penalty area]] during first-half injury time. They extended their lead when striker [[Steven Thompson (Scottish footballer)|Steven Thompson]] converted a [[Penalty shot|penalty]] after McNaughton was felled by [[Clint Easton]]. Hereford scored in the 77th minute through striker [[Theo Robinson]], but they were unable to score an equaliser and Cardiff won the match 2–1 to go through to the fifth round. This was the first time since 1994 that Cardiff had progressed to the fifth round of the FA Cup.<ref>{{cite news|last=Dulin|first=David|title=Hereford 1–2 Cardiff|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/7197727.stm|publisher=BBC Sport|date=27 January 2008|access-date=31 August 2015}}</ref>

Cardiff's fifth round match was at their home ground, [[Ninian Park]], against fellow Championship side [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]]. Whittingham opened the scoring for Cardiff after 90 seconds as he collected a flick-on from [[Jimmy Floyd Hasselbaink]] before shooting past opposition goalkeeper [[Wayne Hennessey]]. Hasselbaink extended Cardiff's advantage nine minutes later by striking the ball into the top-left hand corner of the opposition goal. Wolves were unable to respond in the remainder of the match, their best opportunity fell to centre forward [[Kevin Kyle]] who took advantage of a mistake by centre-back [[Glenn Loovens]] early in the second half, only for Loovens to clear the ball off the goalline. The victory was the furthest Cardiff had advanced in the FA Cup since their victory in the [[1927 FA Cup final|1927 final]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Dulin|first=David|title=Cardiff 2–0 Wolves|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/7224059.stm|publisher=BBC Sport|date=16 February 2008|access-date=31 August 2015}}</ref>

In the sixth round, Cardiff were drawn away to Premier League side [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]] at the [[Riverside Stadium]]. An early goal by Whittingham and a second by defender [[Roger Johnson (footballer)|Roger Johnson]] from a Whittingham free-kick in the 23rd minute was enough to take Cardiff into the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web|last=Dulin|first=David|title=Middlesbrough 0–2 Cardiff|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/7272793.stm|publisher=BBC Sport|date=9 March 2008|access-date=31 August 2015}}</ref> In the semi-final, Cardiff were drawn against Championship side [[Barnsley F.C.|Barnsley]], with both semi-finals being held at [[Wembley Stadium]].<ref>{{harvnb|Saunders|2013|p=299}}</ref> This was the first time since 1908 that three of the four semi-finalists came from outside the top tier of English football, while winning the match was worth £900,000 in prize money.<ref name="draw">{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/7286364.stm |title=FA Cup semi-final draw |publisher=BBC Sport |date=10 March 2008 |access-date=1 October 2020}}</ref> With Cardiff applying early pressure, nine minutes into the game, the ball was cleared from the Barnsley penalty area toward
midfielder [[Joe Ledley]],<ref name="wc167">{{harvnb|Wozencroft|2013|p=167}}</ref> who scored the winning goal with a left-footed volley from outside the area to help Cardiff reach their first FA Cup final since 1927.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2008/apr/06/newsstory.sport9 |title=Cardiff 1–0 Barnsley |newspaper=The Guardian |date=6 April 2008 |access-date=1 October 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Williams|first=Richard|author-link=Richard Williams (journalist)|title=Wheel of fortune turns for Cardiff as Odejayi fails to repeat heroics|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2008/apr/07/sport.sport2|newspaper=The Guardian|date=7 April 2008|access-date=31 August 2015}}</ref>

